note Translated from CPython Lib/concurrent/futures/_base.py Future.set_result (lines 543 to 556).
note Sets the return value of work associated with the future.
note Should only be used by Executor implementations and unit tests.

choice FutureState
    Pending
    Running
    Cancelled
    Cancelled_and_notified
    Finished

note GAP: generics and type parameters - the result can be any Python object; the doc shows `of T` only on a type declaration, so Future of T is used and an operation on it has nowhere to introduce T
type Future of T
    field condition as a Condition
    field state as a FutureState
    field result as a maybe T
    note GAP: shared mutable references - waiters are objects owned elsewhere (one per wait() or as_completed() call) and held here by reference so they can be told about this future; the doc has no shared reference type
    field waiters as a List of shared Waiter
    note GAP: closures and stored callbacks - done callbacks are stored functions run later; the doc has no type for a stored operation
    field done_callbacks as a List of Callback

to add_result future to waiter
    given future is a Future of T
    given waiter is an editable shared Waiter
    uses waiter

to invoke_callbacks future
    given future is a Future of T
    note UNCLEAR: the callbacks can do anything; the doc cannot say "uses whatever the stored callbacks use"
    uses callbacks

note GAP: objects, methods and self - set_result is a method on Future, called from another thread through a shared reference; the doc has no self and treats `editable` as exclusive ownership, which cannot be true for an object two threads hold
to set_result result on future
    given result is a T
    given future is an editable shared Future of T
    fails with InvalidStateError
    note GAP: concurrency, threads and locks - the doc says values are never shared mutably between tasks and there is nothing for a lock to protect; this operation exists to mutate shared state under a lock and wake other threads, so `uses` lists the lock as if it were a capability
    uses condition of future, waiter, callbacks

    note GAP: holding a lock for a block - Python's `with self._condition:` acquires a re-entrant lock and releases it on every exit; `hold` is shown only for files, so `hold ... locked` is a guess at the form
    hold guard as locked condition of future
        note WORKAROUND: Python tests membership in a set literal; the doc shows `is in` only on lists, so a list literal of variants is used
        if state of future is in [Cancelled, Cancelled_and_notified, Finished]
            note GAP: formatted repr of an object in the message - '{}: {!r}'.format(self._state, self) needs a repr of the whole future; the doc's failures carry no message and there is no repr
            fail with InvalidStateError
        set result of future to some result
        set state of future to Finished
        for each waiter in waiters of future
            add_result future to waiter
        note GAP: condition variables - notify_all wakes every thread waiting on the condition; the doc has no wait or notify
        notify_all condition of future
    invoke_callbacks future
